  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "I created the dashboard and the alert rule, and the rule is enabled. Kibana reports its status as `ok`, so it has run at least once and found no spike yet.\n\n**1. The data** (`kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`): about 14,000 web requests dated 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03, so part of the data is in the future. Each record has:\n- the response code, stored as text rather than a number: 200 (12,832), 404 (801) and 503 (441)\n- `bytes`, `url`, `request` and `extension`\n- `clientip`, plus `geo.src`/`geo.dest` and coordinates\n- `machine.os` and `machine.ram`\n- `agent`, `referer` and `tags`\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-99d8b941 Web logs overview\"** (id `eff-99d8b941-web-logs-overview-2ae7b2ecb4f9`, default time range last 7 days), with 7 panels:\n- Total requests\n- Unique visitors (distinct client IPs)\n- Total bytes served\n- Requests over time, split by response code\n- Share of each response code (pie chart)\n- Top 10 URLs, with request count and average bytes\n- Top 10 source countries\n\nLink: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-99d8b941-web-logs-overview-2ae7b2ecb4f9\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-99d8b941 5xx spike\"** (id `5062407c-824a-4b8a-850b-991bab0bc8e5`):\n- It runs every minute on `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- It fires when more than 10 matching documents have a `timestamp` in the last 5 minutes.\n- Because the response code is text, it matches 5xx by looking for codes that start with \"5\". Run against the whole index, that query returns 441 documents, which equals the number of 503s.\n- No action is attached, so when it fires it shows up in Kibana only and doesn't send a notification.",
